# Subagent Model Selection

Model slugs in the table are agent-adjustable per the rule-governance rule (availability
and pricing shift); the cross-family audit rule below is PROTECTED.

## TEMPORARY OVERRIDE — credit burn (active)

**Until the user lifts this override, subagents may ONLY use these two model slugs:**

| Allowed slug | Role under the override |
|---|---|
| `composer-2.5-fast` | Default for exploration, skims, summarization, general implementation, best-of-N, and other non-audit work |
| `cursor-grok-4.5-high` | Hard features / math-heavy work, and all audits / red-teams / reviews |

Do **not** select Claude, GPT, Gemini, or any other slug for subagents while this override
is active — even if the historical defaults table below names them. If a Task tool call
requires a `model` argument, pass one of the two allowed slugs above (or omit/`inherit`
only when the parent is already one of those two).

Cross-family audits under this override: prefer `cursor-grok-4.5-high` to review work
produced by Composer (or by a Claude/GPT parent). Do not use Composer to audit its own
output. Dual independent reviewers are paused until non-Grok families are allowed again.

## Historical defaults (superseded while override is active)

Default choices balancing cost and capability. Start at the cheapest tier that fits the
task; escalate only when a cheaper attempt fails or the task is acceptance-path critical
(spec math, evidence gate, scale/cluster selection). **Ignored while the override above
is active** — remap every row to `composer-2.5-fast` or `cursor-grok-4.5-high` per that
section.

| Task | Model | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Codebase exploration, file finding, "where is X" | `composer-2.5-fast` | Cheap, fast; exploration is retrieval, not reasoning |
| Long-document skims (SI, transcripts), summarization | `gemini-3.5-flash` | Cheap with long context |
| General implementation (well-specified module, tests exist) | `claude-sonnet-5-thinking-high` or `gpt-5.6-terra-medium` | Solid mid-cost coders; enough for spec-following work |
| Parallel best-of-N implementation variants | `gpt-5.6-sol-medium` / `gpt-5.6-terra-medium` | Cheap enough to run several attempts |
| Hard features, tricky refactors, math-heavy spec work (DM gate, dual flow, Q-score, tau* selection) | `claude-fable-5-thinking-high` or `claude-opus-4-8-thinking-high` | Frontier reasoning; acceptance-path code must be right the first time |
| Auditing, red-teaming, reviewing math or proofs | `gpt-5.4-high` or `cursor-grok-4.5-high` | **Cross-family rule below** |
| Whole-corpus consistency passes (paper vs SI vs code vs OPEN_ISSUES) | `gemini-3.1-pro` | Long-context deep analysis across many large files |

## Cross-family audit rule

Never audit an artifact with the same model family that produced it. The parent agent is
Claude, so reviews of Claude-written code/math go to GPT (`gpt-5.4-high`) or Grok
(`cursor-grok-4.5-high`); if a GPT subagent wrote it, review with Claude or Gemini.
For high-stakes derivations (SI lemmas, evidence-gate math), use two independent
reviewers from different families and reconcile disagreements yourself.
**(While the temporary override is active, only Grok is available for audits — see above.)**

## Delegation patterns for this repo

- Broad context gathering: launch `explore` subagents (specify quick/medium/very thorough);
  parallelize independent areas (e.g. one on `code/proteus/src`, one on the SI).
- Narrow questions (one file, one symbol): do it directly; don't spawn a subagent.
- Implementation subagents must be told: the owning SI section, the relevant rules
  (repo-workflow, proteus-implementation, testing-integrity), the `@awaiting` tests they
  should flip, and the exact pytest subset to run. Subagents don't see this conversation.
- Test-weakening is never an acceptable subagent outcome (testing-integrity rule); state
  that explicitly in red-team and implementation prompts.
